WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.271
<v Examiner>Scenario two.

2
00:00:02.271 --> 00:00:05.052
<v Examiner>Leo is four and weighs eighteen kilograms.

3
00:00:05.052 --> 00:00:07.436
<v Examiner>He has a fever and an ear infection.

4
00:00:07.436 --> 00:00:10.548
<v Examiner>His G P prescribes amoxicillin oral suspension,

5
00:00:10.548 --> 00:00:14.389
<v Examiner>and paracetamol fifteen milligrams per kilogram, by mouth,

6
00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:19.819
<v Examiner>every four to six hours as required, no more than four doses in twenty-four hours.

7
00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.587
<v Examiner>His mum, Ana, says she thinks he might be a bit allergic to antibiotics.

8
00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:25.668
<v Examiner>One mark.

9
00:00:25.668 --> 00:00:28.685
<v Examiner>Calculate Leo's single dose of paracetamol.

10
00:00:29.135 --> 00:00:34.502
Fifteen milligrams times eighteen kilograms is two hundred and seventy milligrams.

11
00:00:34.502 --> 00:00:36.793
No working needed, just the answer.

12
00:00:37.243 --> 00:00:37.986
<v Examiner>Two marks.

13
00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:41.403
<v Examiner>Identify two reasons Leo is given paracetamol.

14
00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:46.625
It's an analgesic: it relieves mild to moderate pain, here his sore ear.

15
00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:49.939
And it's an antipyretic: it brings his fever down.

16
00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:50.955
<v Examiner>One mark.

17
00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:54.229
<v Examiner>Why are children prescribed lower doses than adults?

18
00:00:54.679 --> 00:01:00.788
Their liver and kidneys are immature, so they metabolise and excrete drugs more slowly.

19
00:01:00.788 --> 00:01:02.052
Liver, metabolism.

20
00:01:02.052 --> 00:01:03.386
Kidneys, excretion.

21
00:01:03.386 --> 00:01:06.476
Joan said this comes up in State Finals too.

22
00:01:06.926 --> 00:01:07.555
<v Examiner>Two marks.

23
00:01:07.555 --> 00:01:10.828
<v Examiner>Ana says Leo might be a bit allergic to antibiotics.

24
00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:14.542
<v Examiner>What is an allergic reaction, and what should the nurse do?

25
00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:20.449
An allergic reaction is when the body sees the medication as an antigen and mounts an

26
00:01:20.449 --> 00:01:22.182
immune response against it.

27
00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:24.044
That's her formative one key.

28
00:01:24.044 --> 00:01:27.960
The nurse asks exactly what happened last time, documents it,

29
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.555
and tells the prescriber before any penicillin is given.

30
00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:35.664
Antibiotics cause the most allergic reactions of any drug class.

31
00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:36.770
<v Examiner>Two marks.

32
00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:42.407
<v Examiner>Give one piece of safe advice for Ana about storing and giving amoxicillin suspension.

33
00:01:42.857 --> 00:01:47.905
This is her formative one, question nineteen, and any one answer earns the marks.

34
00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:50.585
Store it in the fridge, out of Leo's reach.

35
00:01:50.585 --> 00:01:55.882
Or: finish the whole course, even when he seems better, to fully treat the infection.

36
00:01:55.882 --> 00:02:00.244
Or: measure it with a medicine syringe or cup, not a kitchen teaspoon.

37
00:02:00.694 --> 00:02:01.935
Scenario two, Leo.

38
00:02:01.935 --> 00:02:07.796
Paracetamol fifteen milligrams per kilogram times eighteen is two hundred and seventy

39
00:02:07.796 --> 00:02:08.555
milligrams.

40
00:02:08.555 --> 00:02:11.796
Paracetamol is an analgesic and an antipyretic.

41
00:02:11.796 --> 00:02:16.347
Children get lower doses because of an immature liver and kidneys.

42
00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:21.173
An allergy is the body treating the drug as an antigen; ask, document,

43
00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:22.552
tell the prescriber.

44
00:02:22.552 --> 00:02:27.241
Amoxicillin: fridge, out of reach, finish the course, use a syringe.
